<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Colours available to the Android view layer.

    This file used to hold `purple_200`, `purple_500`, `purple_700`, `teal_200`,
    `teal_700`, `black` and `white` - the stock palette Android Studio writes
    into a new project. Nothing referenced any of them: every colour the app
    draws comes from Color.kt through the Compose theme, and the launcher icon
    carries its own values. They were dead weight from a template that predated
    the Compose theming, and they are gone.

    It is now deliberately empty.

    The launcher icon's blues were briefly duplicated here as `anilist_blue` &
    friends, on the theory that a vector should read them from a colour
    resource. It cannot: a `<vector>` resolves `@color/...` through the
    resource table, but a *gradient* inside it is compiled into the drawable at
    build time and AAPT will not follow a reference into an `<item>` inside a
    `<gradient>`. Lint reported all three as unused, which is the accurate
    verdict - the values had to be literals in the vector regardless. Duplicating
    them here would have created a second copy of the brand palette that could
    drift with nothing to catch it.

    So the palette lives in exactly two places, both of which are literals and
    both of which are commented as such: Color.kt for the running app, and
    ic_launcher_background.xml for the icon.
-->
<resources />